WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.429 --> 00:00:11.919
PAUL JAY: Welcome back to Reality Asserts
Itself on The Real News Network. I'm Paul

00:00:11.919 --> 00:00:12.269
Jay.

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:14.609
We're continuing our series of interviews
with John Kiriakou.

00:00:14.609 --> 00:00:15.459
Thanks for joining us.

00:00:15.459 --> 00:00:16.880
JOHN C. KIRIAKOU: Happy to be here.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.250
JAY: So I've introduced John at the beginning
of every segment. If you look down below the

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:25.579
video player, you will see a whole biography.
So I'm not going to do it again.

00:00:25.579 --> 00:00:32.579
Okay. So you leave Bahrain, and you've started
understanding that this fight in Bahrain,

00:00:33.699 --> 00:00:38.719
as you've just put it in the last segment,
was really rich versus poor. This whole thing

00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:45.719
about it being fundamentally religious, Shia-Sunni,
was not true, that this was terrible violations

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:50.739
of human rights. And even though you could
see it and the U.S. ambassador could see it

00:00:50.739 --> 00:00:56.079
and you were reporting back to the State Department
and you're CIA, and, obviously, back to the

00:00:56.079 --> 00:01:02.649
president, the Fifth Fleet, port of the Fifth
Fleet, and the arms sales trump the human

00:01:02.649 --> 00:01:04.960
rights issues, and so on and so on.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:11.420
So you start--it starts to eat at a bit of
your Americanism, and you leave and you go

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:18.360
back to Arlington, and you're back on the
Iraq file. This is at the height of sanctions.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:24.369
So a question I've always had about this period
is: why didn't the United States give a damn,

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:30.890
in the sense of leave Saddam alone? There's
dictators all over the world; who cares about

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:36.299
this one? So he suppresses his people; that's
never been a big deal for the United States,

00:01:36.299 --> 00:01:42.100
really. He still has to sell his oil on the
world market; you have access to the oil.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:43.950
What is all this sanctions and everything
about?

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:48.409
KIRIAKOU: It was a pissing match between Saddam
Hussein and a series of American presidents

00:01:48.409 --> 00:01:54.600
is what it really came down to. The first
President Bush, President Clinton, the second

00:01:54.600 --> 00:02:00.970
President Bush wanted Saddam to do what they
told him to do, and he was unwilling to just

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:01.579
fall in line.

00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:03.390
JAY: So he had to say uncle.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:04.799
KIRIAKOU: He had to say uncle.

00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:07.960
JAY: So how many people died 'cause he would
say uncle?

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:11.730
KIRIAKOU: Oh, untold tens of thousands of
people died. Whether it was as a result of

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:18.730
sanctions or fighting or American airstrikes
or whatever it was, thousands and thousands

00:02:20.989 --> 00:02:27.739
of people died just because of this personality
conflict, for lack of a better term, between

00:02:27.739 --> 00:02:30.029
Saddam Hussein and three American presidents.

00:02:30.029 --> 00:02:36.769
JAY: It's crazy. There's an interview with
Lyndon Johnson near the end of his presidency

00:02:36.769 --> 00:02:43.519
in the Vietnam War, and he's asked, why do
you keep continuing this? What is this about?

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:48.609
And he actually, apparently, pulls down his
fly and brings out his organ--as this is how

00:02:48.609 --> 00:02:53.699
it's described by one of his biographers--and
he says, this is what it's about.

00:02:53.699 --> 00:02:57.069
KIRIAKOU: I believe that story.

00:02:57.069 --> 00:03:04.069
JAY: At the time, how much do you understand
that's what it's about, that it's just a pissing

00:03:04.459 --> 00:03:04.709
match?

00:03:04.459 --> 00:03:09.400
KIRIAKOU: I did understand it, and I grew
frustrated with it. I grew frustrated with

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:15.379
American policy toward Iraq and decided I've
got to do something completely different.

00:03:15.379 --> 00:03:17.419
And that's when I began looking for new job.

00:03:17.419 --> 00:03:18.359
JAY: Within the CIA.

00:03:18.359 --> 00:03:19.349
KIRIAKOU: Within the CIA.

00:03:19.349 --> 00:03:20.519
JAY: And you go to Greece.

00:03:20.519 --> 00:03:27.519
KIRIAKOU: Well, there was a position advertised
that called for either a Greek or Arabic speaker.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.709
And it turned out that at the time--.

00:03:33.709 --> 00:03:38.269
JAY: You know what? I'm sorry. I want to go
back to where you said you can believe the

00:03:38.269 --> 00:03:39.459
Johnson story.

00:03:39.459 --> 00:03:45.069
Alright. So you're a professional analyst.
You're analyzing what's going on in Iraq,

00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:48.069
what should be done. I mean, it sounds like
you're coming to the conclusion, like, all

00:03:48.069 --> 00:03:55.069
of this is unnecessary in terms of real U.S.
national interest. You're saying this is essentially

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:00.589
a pissing match. I mean, and I don't think
we should make that too banal. What I mean

00:04:00.589 --> 00:04:06.279
by that: it isn't just a personality thing.
I think ingrained in U.S. foreign policy is

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:12.309
this, that we must make everyone believe we
are stronger than they are. And it's sort

00:04:12.309 --> 00:04:17.190
of like a loan shark. I said this in another
interview. If you let someone get away with

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:21.709
not paying back their interest that week,
then everyone else isn't going to pay back.

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:26.509
That's the theory. So you've got to break
some knees, and if somebody's really defiant,

00:04:26.509 --> 00:04:32.210
for that, for its own sake, you have to prove
you can put that person in their place.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:35.009
But, as an analyst, you can see this isn't
good foreign policy.

00:04:35.009 --> 00:04:39.930
KIRIAKOU: No, it was quite bad foreign policy.
It was a waste of resources and people were

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:46.569
getting killed. But at the same time, it goes
beyond the president and the State Department

00:04:46.569 --> 00:04:50.300
and the Defense Department. You have congressional
leaders hammering the president for being

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:56.639
weak on Iraq and to bomb more and to fight
harder and to make sure that Saddam is humiliated.

00:04:56.639 --> 00:05:03.639
And so you have this spiral of bad policy
that you just can't get out of.

00:05:03.949 --> 00:05:08.180
JAY: And how much do you think that for certain
sectors of the economy--'cause it's certainly

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:13.520
not true for all of the economy, but if you're
in fossil fuels or if you're in military production

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:17.800
and associated high tech, war's damn good
for business.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:23.500
KIRIAKOU: It is good for business. And when
you think about it, though, if we--. Look

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:29.050
at it this way. We bought much, much more
Libyan oil than we ever bought Iraqi oil.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:34.550
Iraqi oil mostly went to Europe. And when
Libya collapsed and their oil industry came

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:41.189
to a screeching halt, it had virtually no
effect on our own economy. Virtually none.

00:05:41.189 --> 00:05:47.060
So did we really need to hammer the Iraqis
like this over more than a decade to protect

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:49.280
the oil? We really didn't need the oil anyway.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:54.289
JAY: But by fossil fuel I mean as long as
there's conflict, the price of oil's high.

00:05:54.289 --> 00:05:54.919
KIRIAKOU: Mhm. It stays high.

00:05:54.919 --> 00:05:58.759
JAY: We know big oil companies make more money
the higher the price of oil.

00:05:58.759 --> 00:05:59.330
KIRIAKOU: That's right.

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:03.030
JAY: People selling arms, the more stuff you
blow up, the more stuff you've got to buy

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:05.870
to replace it, and the more threat of conflict,
the more--.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:06.629
KIRIAKOU: Right. It's good for business.

00:06:06.629 --> 00:06:08.370
JAY: How much do you think that drives U.S.
foreign policy?

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:13.159
KIRIAKOU: I think that's an integral part
of U.S. foreign policy. I really do. You know,

00:06:13.159 --> 00:06:18.699
we've got not just arms manufacturers, but
now we have drone manufacturers, for example,

00:06:18.699 --> 00:06:23.999
that are having to compete against Israeli
drones and Chinese drones and Russian drones.

00:06:23.999 --> 00:06:29.939
So we need for there to be conflicts so we
can sell our drones. It's the same with aircraft.

00:06:29.939 --> 00:06:36.939
You know, Boeing and other aircraft manufacturers
would go under if we couldn't sell F-15s and

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:44.039
F-16s and F-whatever they are, 23s, the new
ones that are coming out, both for our own

00:06:44.039 --> 00:06:48.159
military and for foreign militaries. So war
is good for business.

00:06:48.159 --> 00:06:52.349
JAY: I mean, if you're thinking of the current
situation, the more potential conflict there

00:06:52.349 --> 00:06:56.819
is between the Saudis and the Iranians, that's
a gold mine If you're selling arms.

00:06:56.819 --> 00:07:01.629
KIRIAKOU: Especially when the Saudis have
a bottomless pit of money that they can dip

00:07:01.629 --> 00:07:06.740
into. The same with the Qataris and the Emiratis.
It's very lucrative for us to be in the Gulf.

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:13.580
JAY: Now, let's go back. As you're leaving,
you go back to Arlington. You're back on the

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:18.509
Iraq file. You're starting to see how crazy
all this stuff is. Are you starting to question

00:07:18.509 --> 00:07:18.969
now?

00:07:18.969 --> 00:07:24.490
KIRIAKOU: Yeah, now I'm starting to get frustrated.
This policy is broken, it's not working, and

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:28.639
there's no hope of changing it. So I decided
to do something completely different.

00:07:28.639 --> 00:07:29.219
JAY: Okay.

00:07:29.219 --> 00:07:30.849
KIRIAKOU: And that was operations.

00:07:30.849 --> 00:07:37.560
JAY: So--oh. Now you're going to leave analysis
go to operations. Now, this to me sounds a

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:43.819
little contradictory. You're starting to see
the pattern of some of the underlining rot

00:07:43.819 --> 00:07:48.050
of the policy, but now you're going to go
over to operations, where some of the dark

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:48.879
stuff gets done.

00:07:48.879 --> 00:07:52.789
KIRIAKOU: Yeah, but some of the dark stuff
was meant to save and to protect American

00:07:52.789 --> 00:07:56.360
lives, and that's really what I wanted to
focus on.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:00.770
I ended up going to Greece and spending two
years in Greece. And my job in Greece was

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:07.770
to try to disrupt terrorist attacks committed
by a group that was called Revolutionary Organization

00:08:08.559 --> 00:08:15.069
17 November. 17 November had murdered the
CIA station chief in Athens in 1975. They

00:08:15.069 --> 00:08:20.990
murdered two defense attaches. They had shot
and severely wounded several embassy officers.

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:26.900
And they murdered an American Air Force technical
sergeant who was just--the poor guy was just

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:33.779
in the wrong place at the wrong time. And
they had murdered almost two dozen Greek nationals

00:08:33.779 --> 00:08:40.779
as well, important people--cabinet ministers,
the heads of the central bank, university

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:45.820
professors, prominent business leaders. And
I thought, this is something I could sink

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:46.660
my teeth into.

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:49.970
JAY: But when you decide to join ops, you
don't know that's where you're going.

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:50.750
KIRIAKOU: Oh, yeah.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:51.130
JAY: You do?

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:51.510
KIRIAKOU: Oh, yeah.

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:52.220
JAY: Oh, you know it's Greece.

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:54.240
KIRIAKOU: I applied specifically for that
job.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:55.709
JAY: And what's the training?

00:08:55.709 --> 00:09:00.899
KIRIAKOU: It was all of the traditional operational
training at--.

00:09:00.899 --> 00:09:03.279
JAY: Tradecraft they call it. Is that right?

00:09:03.279 --> 00:09:03.529
KIRIAKOU: Tradecraft, right,--

00:09:03.329 --> 00:09:03.579
JAY: Yeah.

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:09.589
KIRIAKOU: --at a facility they call "the Farm",
which is located south of here.

00:09:09.589 --> 00:09:13.190
JAY: And how long is the training?

00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:16.589
KIRIAKOU: Well, because I was midcareer, I
didn't have to go through what they called

00:09:16.589 --> 00:09:23.589
CIA 101. So I went straight into the shooting
and the car crashing and the explosives training.

00:09:23.779 --> 00:09:29.610
And that lasted four and a half months.

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:32.800
JAY: What was the most interesting of that
training?

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:39.800
KIRIAKOU: I found that I had a real knack
with firearms. On my first day in the firearms

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:47.130
or the weapons training class, the instructor
asked, who in the room does not own a gun?

00:09:48.029 --> 00:09:53.290
And I put my hand up and I looked around,
and mine was the only hand up. And he said,

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:58.699
you don't own a gun? And I said, truth be
told, I've never actually touched a real gun.

00:09:58.699 --> 00:10:04.750
And he said, oh my God, we've got to start
at the beginning. Well, I ended up finishing

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:09.310
the class with the highest score. I had a
steady hand, and I just had a knack for it.

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:16.310
And then we took a class called "Counterterrorist
Driving". I had a knack for that, too--you

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:23.810
know, 60 miles an hour backwards, crashing
through roadblocks. You learn a lot, and it

00:10:24.779 --> 00:10:26.709
could potentially save your life.

00:10:26.709 --> 00:10:31.170
JAY: Now, when you're learning how to shoot,
you realize you're learning how to shoot people,--

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:31.470
KIRIAKOU: Absolutely.

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:32.709
JAY: --not to shoot targets.

00:10:32.709 --> 00:10:34.089
KIRIAKOU: Absolutely.

00:10:34.089 --> 00:10:35.480
JAY: You're okay with that.

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:40.490
KIRIAKOU: I had to be. If somebody puts a
gun in my face with the specific intent to

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:43.220
blow my brains out, I'm going to kill him
first.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:47.889
JAY: But that's certainly not all. I mean,
CIA is known for assassinating and shooting

00:10:47.889 --> 00:10:49.959
people who aren't necessarily shooting at
you.

00:10:49.959 --> 00:10:56.610
KIRIAKOU: Completely, totally different situation.
That was not my thing.

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:58.320
JAY: But you could be ordered to do that.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:10:59.790
KIRIAKOU: No, I could not be ordered to do
it.

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:00.070
JAY: Why?

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:03.120
KIRIAKOU: Because I wasn't trained for it.
And it's not something you can be ordered

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:09.000
to do. In the event that there's an assassination
that takes place under these new executive

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:14.810
orders that presidents Bush and Obama have
signed, there's a paramilitary group that's

00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:20.009
perfectly willing to do that. That's not what
the average Joe case officer does. The average

00:11:20.009 --> 00:11:26.420
Joe case officer, of which I was one, is overseas
to recruit spies to steal secrets. That's

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:27.209
the job.

00:11:27.209 --> 00:11:28.860
JAY: So the gun, in theory, is--

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:29.449
KIRIAKOU: It was for self-defense.

00:11:29.449 --> 00:11:31.220
JAY: --to defend yourself if they figure out--

00:11:31.220 --> 00:11:31.550
KIRIAKOU: Yeah.

00:11:31.550 --> 00:11:32.440
JAY: --they're coming after you.

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:33.079
KIRIAKOU: It was for self-defense.

00:11:33.079 --> 00:11:33.990
JAY: Which in Greece they did.

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:35.019
KIRIAKOU: They did.

00:11:35.019 --> 00:11:37.740
JAY: There was attempts to shoot you. [incompr.]

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:39.329
KIRIAKOU: There was an attempt.

00:11:39.329 --> 00:11:41.110
JAY: What happened?

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:47.500
KIRIAKOU: Well, in the end, instead of killing
me, the group killed the British defense attaché

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:51.470
who was a neighbor of mine. Our backyards
connected.

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:58.470
And I left for work one morning a little bit
later than I normally did. I tried to leave

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:02.449
at a different time every day, and I took
a different route every day, but I slept in

00:12:02.449 --> 00:12:08.370
this one day. And so I thought, ah, damn it,
I'll just get on the main road, which you're

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:11.120
never supposed to do, and just go straight
to the embassy.

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:17.949
My house was in a neighborhood called Kifissia,
which is exactly ten miles north of the center

00:12:17.949 --> 00:12:23.829
of Athens. And there's a main road, Kifissias
boulevard, that goes straight from Kifissia

00:12:23.829 --> 00:12:27.420
directly to the embassy. You never have to
get off the road. So, of course, that's the

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:32.560
road I never wanted to take, because there
are Jersey barriers on both sides, and if

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:37.449
somebody was going to kill me, there's nowhere
for me to run. I can't crash through the concrete

00:12:37.449 --> 00:12:42.790
barrier to get away. So I would always take
neighborhood routes all the way into town.

00:12:42.790 --> 00:12:48.459
But I slept in this one day, and I thought,
I don't have the extra 30 minutes to wind

00:12:48.459 --> 00:12:51.860
my way through the neighborhood; I'm just
going to get on Kifissias boulevard. But it's

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:58.860
a parking lot. And, you know, Athens has crazy
traffic, you know, Cairo, Bangkok kind of

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:07.430
traffic, but this was heavier than even that.
So I put on the radio and I heard an announcement

00:13:10.019 --> 00:13:16.399
to avoid Kifissias boulevard because there's
a traffic incident at Psychiko, which is closer

00:13:16.399 --> 00:13:21.209
to the city. And I thought, boy, I've never
heard an announcement like that. But I'm already

00:13:21.209 --> 00:13:26.720
committed to Kifissias boulevard, and I can't
get off 'cause of these Jersey barriers. So

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:31.240
I stayed on, and I'm inching my way down the
mountain into Athens.

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:31.790
And then about--.

00:13:31.790 --> 00:13:32.870
JAY: You're alone in the car?

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:34.019
KIRIAKOU: Alone in the car.

00:13:34.019 --> 00:13:35.339
JAY: Why?

00:13:35.339 --> 00:13:42.339
KIRIAKOU: I was always alone in the car. Armored
car. Fully, heavily armored. And I have two

00:13:43.779 --> 00:13:50.779
guns on me. And in case everything goes to
shit, I have a switchblade as well.

00:13:52.639 --> 00:13:58.009
So about a half an hour later I hear, "Avoid
Kifissias boulevard because there's a police

00:13:58.009 --> 00:14:04.730
incident at Psychiko." And I thought, that's
odd. I wonder what that means? And then, finally,

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:10.269
another half hour into it, "Avoid Kifissias
boulevard because there's a terrorist incident

00:14:10.269 --> 00:14:13.600
at Psychiko." And I thought, oh, for God's
sake.

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:20.600
So I'm inching my way down the mountain, and
I see a white car, a Rover, a British Rover,

00:14:21.410 --> 00:14:28.319
and all of the glass is blown out, and there's
what looks like blood on the door and on the

00:14:28.319 --> 00:14:35.319
hood. And I looked at the license plate number,
and it started with YBH. Well, all of our

00:14:36.449 --> 00:14:42.250
license plates began with the letters YHB,
and I thought, oh my God, some poor Greek

00:14:42.250 --> 00:14:49.250
was killed because 17 November thought it
was one of our cars. But then I remember that

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:56.220
YBH was the British Embassy's license plate
numbers, and I recognize the Rover as belonging

00:14:56.220 --> 00:15:02.079
to Stephen Saunders, the newly arrived British
defense attaché.

00:15:02.079 --> 00:15:08.670
So I called the office and I told them what
I had seen. They didn't know what it was.

00:15:08.670 --> 00:15:11.610
They knew something had happened, but they
didn't know what it was. I said, it's Stephen

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:16.800
Saunders' car. I said, there's blood everywhere.
So they called the British Embassy. The British

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:22.100
said he had been shot. It turned out he'd
been shot with a G3, with a tank-piercing

00:15:22.100 --> 00:15:27.870
round. It blew his hand completely off. And
then he was shot multiple times with the same

00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:33.620
gun that was used to kill Dick Welch, our
station chief, in 1975, called the Welch .45.

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:40.620
17 November used the Welch .45 in most of
